. /// extra filter for H3
> /*
> The household identifier does not uniquely identify set of parents. So we kee
> p only observations with maximum two parents in one year per household. Each 
> parent may have daughter born in different year, we keep the year of the 'old
> er' daughter.  
> */
. gen p =1

. bys hid syear: egen np = sum(p)

. bys hid: egen mnp = max(np)

. 
. drop if mnp > 2
(10,774 observations deleted)

. 
. bys hid: egen _min = min(firstgirl)

. bys hid: egen _max = max(firstgirl)

. g firstgirl2=0

. replace firstgirl2=_min if _min~=0
(28,696 real changes made)

. replace firstgirl2=_max if firstgirl2==0
(6,700 real changes made)

. drop _* p np mnp

. 
. collapse (mean) gender_score1 firstgirl2 , by(hid syear)

. 
. 
. /*
> gen n = 1 if firstgirl == 0
> bys hid: egen sn = sum(n)
> bys hid: egen firstgirl_test = max(firstgirl)
> replace firstgirl2 = firstgirl_test if sn != 0 & firstgirl2 == 0
> corr firstgirl2 _firstgirl
> sort hid pid syear 
> bro sex hid pid gebjahr syear first* p np mnp n sn if sn != 0 & firstgirl2 ==
>  0 & firstgirl_test>0
> bro sex hid pid gebjahr syear first* p np mnp n sn kids* if sn~=0 //firstgirl
> _test>0
> drop n np mnp
> */
. 
. * GENERATE VARIABLES * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. /*
> The analysis is at household level, so we collapse the dependent variable to 
> household level. and take the year of birth of oldest daughter in the househo
> ld as treatment period. 
> */
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. * H3: Having daughters leads to more gender equality in the household (as mea
> sured by behaviors or outcomes).
. 
.         * Please add a verbal description of your model here
. /*
> We use the measure of gender equality 'gender_score1' --- index adopted from 
> Wandschneider et al. (2022) as dependent variable ranging from 0 to 1, with 1
>  representing a belief in more differentiated gender roles (behavior/outcome)
>  --- as dependent variable.
> The model is basic two-way-fixed-effects model (TWFE). Treatment group are pa
> rents (pid) with daughter(s). The year starting from the year a girl is born 
> is the treatment period (firstgirl). 
> Method: Callaway and Sant'Anna (2020) staggared diff-diff estimator. Control 
> group: never treated and not-yet-treated. We provide the ATT after treatment 
> as main result --- _b[Post_avg]. 
> H3: having daughters leads to higher outcomes, i.e. positive coefficient 
> */      
