. * EXTRACT AND EXPORT RESULTS * (Please check whether the suggested code autom
> atically picks up the correct statistics for your chosen regression analysis 
> and adapt if needed)
. 
.         * Extract the required statistics
. 
.                 * (i) coefficient estimate
.                 *local coefficient = _b[Post_avg]
.                 local coefficient = b[1,2]

.                 * (ii) standard error
.                 *local se = _se[Post_avg]
.                 local se = b[2,2]

.                 * (iii) family of the test statistic (e.g. z, t, F, Chi²)
.                 local test_stat_family = "z"

. 
.                 * (iv) residual and model degrees of freedom (DFs) if applica
> ble
.                 *local df_residual = e(df_r)
.                 *local df_model = e(df_m)
. 
.                 * (v) p-value
.                 local p_value = b[4,2]

.                 
.                 * (vi) number of observations
.                 local num_obs = num_obs

. 
.                 * (vii) number of participants
.                 local num_participants = num_participants

. 
.                 * (viii) interpretation of directionality // Adapted by DA du
> ring consistency check
.                 //local interpretation "not linearly interpretable"
.                 * Set interpretation based on mean
.                 if `mean_gender_score1' > 0.5 {
.         local interpretation "negative coefficient is in line with hypothesis
> "
.                 }

.                 if `mean_gender_score1' == 0.5 {
.         local interpretation "not linearly interpretable"
.                 }

.                 else {
.         local interpretation "positive coefficient is in line with hypothesis
> "
.                 }

.                 /* excerpt from Wandschneider et al.: "the higher the gender 
> score of a participant
>                 is, the higher the levels of gendered practices and attitudes
>                 associated with being female are. In line with the original w
> orks
>                 of Pelletier et al., we described the continuum ranging from
>                 “masculine” (towards zero) to “feminine” (towards 1) gendered
>                 social practices, with “androgynous” gendered practices inbet
> ween
>                 the two poles indicating balanced levels of masculine
>                 and feminine gendered social practices (12)."*/
.                 
. 
.         // Display the saved statistics
.         di "Coefficient: `coefficient'"
Coefficient: .1123834130966768

.         di "Standard Error: `se'"
Standard Error: .0169836147340802

.         di "Test Statistic Family: `test_stat_family'"
Test Statistic Family: z

.         di "Residual Degrees of Freedom: `df_residual'"
